select $q$Pharmacology$q$,$q$mcq$q$,$q$A client receiving insulin reports shakiness and sweating. The glucose is 52 mg/dL. The client is alert and able to swallow. Which action should the nurse plan first?$q$,$q$[{"id":"a","text":"Offer peanut butter crackers"},{"id":"b","text":"Administer glucagon intramuscularly"},{"id":"c","text":"Give 15 to 20 g of a fast-acting carbohydrate such as 4 oz of juice"},{"id":"d","text":"Recheck the glucose in one hour"}]$q$::jsonb,$q${"ids":["c"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$An alert client who can swallow should receive a fast-acting carbohydrate, with the glucose rechecked in about 15 minutes. Glucagon is for clients who cannot swallow safely, and fat-containing snacks absorb too slowly.$q$,'draft',$q$Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies$q$,$q$Generate Solutions$q$,'pending',$q$diagnostic-parts-b-d-2026-09$q$,$q$DRAFT for clinical review. Requires an approving clinical reviewer and structured references before publication. Diagnostic B23 (Part B); intended difficulty easy; subtopic: Hypoglycemia treatment.$q$,$q$An alert client who can swallow should receive a fast-acting carbohydrate, with the glucose rechecked in about 15 minutes. Glucagon is for clients who cannot swallow safely, and fat-containing snacks absorb too slowly.$q$,$q${"a":{"is_correct":false,"explanation":"Fat and protein slow absorption, so crackers with peanut butter are not the first treatment."},"b":{"is_correct":false,"explanation":"Glucagon is for clients who cannot swallow safely or are unresponsive."},"c":{"is_correct":true,"explanation":"A responsive client who can swallow needs fast-acting carbohydrate, then a recheck in about 15 minutes."},"d":{"is_correct":false,"explanation":"Waiting an hour leaves symptomatic hypoglycemia untreated."}}$q$::jsonb
